Prajwalit

Local preview
  python3 -m http.server 8081 --bind 127.0.0.1
  Open http://localhost:8081

Static GitHub Pages site; no build step.
Scroll, or use “Start the journey”, to fly up an imagined mountain fjord.
Five chapters: introduction, current work, career background, journeys, and
the summit. The terrain is intentionally sculptural, with coral sunset light,
cool mountain silhouettes, reflective water, and a continuous camera route
toward a summit.

The central arm is a fjord, not a lake: roughly 5,000 units long and 900 to
2,000 wide, walled by slopes that rise 200 to 1,100 straight off the shore,
and open at its seaward end to water that runs past the terrain to the
horizon. About 65% of the mapped area lies below the waterline.

No photographs, satellite imagery, or Trolltunga reconstruction appear in the
landscape; the only photograph on the site is the portrait in the about dialog.

Implementation
  scripts/journey.js — deterministic procedural terrain, sky shader,
    planar-reflection water, scroll camera, chapter fades, and dialogs. The
    only place a renderer, light or render target is created.
  scripts/sky-shader.js — sky, clouds, stars, Milky Way, moon and aurora, all
    in one fragment pass, so the water reflection carries every one of them.
  scripts/sunset.js — the day cycle. DAY_CYCLE_DURATION lives here.
  scripts/vegetation.js — instanced conifers and grass, with slope/height
    limits and roots sampled from the rendered terrain triangles. Meadow
    colour fades into exposed rock higher up; trees become smaller and
    sparser at the treeline.
  scripts/aurora.js — when the aurora may start, and how long it lasts.
  scripts/pointer.js — the shared release-gesture and dismiss-on-outside-press
    helpers used by the overlays and every dialog.
  scripts/seeded-random.js — the one deterministic hash behind procedural
    placement, so a seed gives the same detail in every module.
  styles/journey.css — desktop/mobile layout with fixed chapter panels.
  assets/water-normal.jpg — water normal map from the Three.js examples.
  assets/vendor — locally vendored Three.js 0.170.0 and Water; MIT license.
    Water includes a local extension for multisampled, mipmapped reflections
    and viewport-aware render-target sizing (long edge capped at 1536).
    Subpixel ripples fade with screen-space footprint; the 10-unit camera
    near plane improves distant shoreline depth precision.
  Typography — Google Fonts: Italiana and DM Sans.

Accessibility and fallbacks
  Reduced-motion preferences disable ambient animation and camera smoothing,
  start the panorama and the sky clock paused, hide birds and shooting stars,
  hold the aurora still, and make returns instant.
  Inactive panels are inert and hidden. Dialogs use native modal focus
  handling and share one dismissal gesture: a press that both starts and ends
  outside the panel, so dragging a dial, preview or control past the edge
  never closes it.
  Manual scrolling, touch, or key input stops automatic playback.
  If WebGL cannot initialize, the text becomes a normal readable page.

The journey
  One continuous route from the water to a summit panorama, 75 seconds end to
  end under automatic playback. The summit is derived from the actual central
  terrain vertices. The camera uses terrain clearance during ascent and
  settles above the peak, holding a level gaze so the horizon rests on the
  centre line throughout the turn.
  At the summit the view turns at one revolution every 90 seconds. Scrolling
  away stops the turn; dialogs and background tabs pause its clock. Horizontal
  trackpad scrolling, or Shift with a mouse wheel, pans the view and pauses
  the automatic turn, and Resume panorama continues from that heading.
  Vertical scrolling still moves back down the journey.

Free exploration
  Explore freely opens a bounded free-flight camera. Drag to look; WASD or
  arrows move relative to the view, E/Q rise and descend. The on-screen
  joystick and +/- controls work with pointer and touch input.
  The key badges name WASD and E/Q themselves, so the hint carries only the
  gesture nothing labels, “Drag to look”, which sits under the badges on a
  mouse and under the title on touch. That leaves “A view of your own.” alone
  in the middle of the view.
  Escape or Return to summit restores the guided departure pose in 1.8
  seconds, instantly with reduced motion. Terrain clearance samples rendered
  triangles; altitude and horizontal bounds keep the camera above the water
  and inside the scenery. Inputs clear on blur, hidden tabs, pointer
  cancellation, and dialogs. The guided journey stays at the departure point
  while exploring; returning leaves automatic motion paused. No wake lock is
  held in free exploration, and the music preference is preserved.

  Bounds: x ±7,800; z -13,500 to +4,200; altitude cap 3,300. The extended
  terrain grid preserves the original spacing and vertex alignment, with
  clearance sampled from the same visible mesh throughout the larger area.

  Three ways in. The compass beside the place name opens exploration from any
  point, including the beginning, and the line under the place name names
  that action rather than restating the scroll; at the summit that line
  returns to the sideways pan hint, where the Explore freely button is
  already on screen. The button itself appears at the summit. And at the
  summit alone, W A S D or Q E enter flight directly, as does dragging the
  scenery past twelve pixels — the gesture carries over, so the press that
  asked to fly is the one that starts moving or looking.
  Arrows, space and touch stay page scrolling, or there would be no way back
  down, and text and controls keep their own gestures. Neither of those last
  two entries starts the soundtrack: only an explicit control may do that.
  Return to journey restores the point of departure; summit entry still
  returns to the summit.

Day and night cycle
  A 48-minute cycle begins at the opening sunset, passes through moonlit
  night with stars and a soft Milky Way band, then sunrise and daylight,
  before returning to sunset. It pauses while the tab is hidden. Reduced
  motion keeps the opening sky until a visitor changes it.
  Celestial detail uses the existing sky shader and water reflection. The one
  shadowed directional light alternates between sun and moon; no extra
  lights, textures or rendering passes are added. Ambient light keeps night
  navigable. Sun and moon motion uses continuous positive tangents across
  phases and the loop seam.
  Stars and the Milky Way rotate together around a tilted celestial axis,
  once per cycle. Atmospheric clouds and the horizon remain independent. The
  galaxy has variable width, an asymmetric core and fragmented dust lanes.
  The sunset update returns the state it has already computed, and the frame
  threads night through to the discoveries; read the cycle from that return
  rather than calling dayCycleState again.

  Time controls. The moon icon beside Save a view opens a dial and a
  pause/resume control. The dial traces the sun's own arc as you would watch
  it facing the equator: sunrise on the left, noon at the top, sunset on the
  right, midnight underneath. Positions come straight from the solar angle,
  so the markers cannot drift out of step with the sky. Selecting a time smoothly changes the light and holds
  it until Resume cycle, at speeds from 1x to 16x. Dragging a control never
  starts a text selection: the dial, the wallpaper preview and the flight
  surfaces all opt out, and the dial also suppresses the press's default,
  focusing itself explicitly instead so its arrow keys still work. Sunset lasts the first
  eight minutes of the loop. Camera and music controls remain independent.

Aurora
  One aurora a night, starting between 23:00 and 02:00. Solar angle fixes the
  hours: it is PI at midnight, so an hour is PI/12, which puts 23:00 at 0.3014
  of the cycle and 02:00 at 0.3973. Arriving anywhere in those hours starts
  it, including by dragging the dial there. It plays once and then stays dark,
  re-arming only after the sky has left those hours with nothing running, so
  holding the dial inside them cannot loop it and a fast sky cannot stack a
  second one on the first.
  The hours decide when it may start, not when it must stop. Once begun it
  runs its full 144 seconds in real time, the length of Cloud Valley, so the
  piece underneath always finishes whether the sky is paused or at 16x. At 1x
  it ends around 00:45, well inside the night. A sky running fast carries the
  event past sunrise so the music is never cut, but the sky's own darkness
  still decides what is seen: the term is multiplied by night, so a sky run
  round to daylight shows nothing while the piece plays out.
  Drawn inside the existing sky pass, so it adds no geometry, light, render
  target or texture, and the fjord reflects it. Projecting onto a plane far
  overhead makes the curtains converge toward the zenith and compress toward
  the horizon; warping the across-coordinate drapes them into sheets rather
  than stripes.
  Colour comes from two things. Intensity sets the body: violet through the
  faint diffuse parts, oxygen green through the brighter curtain, a pale wash
  in the cores, with violet also painted into the surround because the dim
  end of a ramp goes black. Height then stacks nitrogen magenta above the
  green, the way a strong display shows it. The ramp runs over d.y 0.12 to
  0.38 at 0.55 strength, which averages close to 30% magenta across the
  visible sky and leaves 70% green. The level summit view spans only d.y 0
  to about 0.45, so the ramp has to sit inside that: pitched higher the pink
  lands off the top of the frame, pitched lower it tints everything.
  A diffuse glow along the base keeps the ribbons standing on a lit horizon
  instead of converging into a bare wedge.
  The whole term rides a uAurora uniform, so outside those hours it costs
  nothing at all. Reduced motion holds it still, as the cloud clock is what
  moves it.
  It uses its own hash and its own clock, both for mobile precision. The
  shared hash feeds its coordinate into sin(), which mobile GPUs evaluate
  badly once the argument is large; by the aurora's hours it was past 9,000
  and a whole lattice cell collapsed to a single value, which showed as flat
  facets across the sky. auroraHash never leaves [0,1) after its first fract
  and holds up at any precision, and uAuroraTime restarts with each event so
  the coordinate stays under 25 rather than climbing with uTime all session.
  Stars, clouds and the Milky Way still use the shared hash.

Music
  Silent Trail is the background: a 108-second, volume-reduced AAC clip that
  loops and attempts to play on page load. Browsers that block autoplay show
  a start control. The mute preference is saved in local storage.
  Cloud Valley, 143.6 seconds and fading out on its own, plays under the
  aurora and hands back afterwards, crossfading over 150 frames. The handback
  happens on whichever comes first, the track reaching its own end or the
  window closing; crossfading to the track already playing is a no-op.
  Whichever piece comes in starts at its opening, so the soundtrack returns
  from the top rather than from wherever it paused. Tab switches resume
  instead, and the render loop stops the clock while hidden, so the event and
  its music stay in step.
  Cloud Valley is preload="none", so the 2.3MB is only fetched on a night
  that reaches 23:00. Both tracks follow the one mute preference, and a muted
  element still runs, which is what makes unmuting mid-piece instant rather
  than silent.

Landscape discoveries
  A cairn sits on a western ridge. Wildflowers grow on the opening shoreline
  near x 576 / z 984, about 15 units above the water, close to the early
  route. Ground discoveries use actual terrain samples and distance culling,
  and total about 31,300 instanced triangles across the map. No added lights
  or shadow casters.

  Wildflowers. Small cream and dusty pink cups, basal leaves and varied stem
  heights, planted against the actual terrain. The opening patch is culled
  beyond 400 units and adds no image download. Fifteen more grow on
  meadow-compatible lower slopes and fjord banks, between elevations 25 and
  650, each with 30–54 flowers of varied orientation, height and cluster
  size — sixteen locations in all. They share two tiny geometry buffers and
  are culled beyond 280 units; their centres are more than 600 units apart,
  so at most one added patch renders at a time, two draw calls, which
  reflections may draw again.

  Birds. Seven distant birds cross the visible sky in a loose flock, fading
  in and out over an 18-second crossing, then every 120 seconds. The flock
  waits for clear sky and keeps its chosen world-space route even if the
  camera turns. The first waits for the camera to come to rest, at the summit
  or in free exploration, and crosses ten seconds later: the guided route
  takes 75 seconds, so arriving by autoplay brings one at about 86. It is
  never later than the standing 120, so a visitor who neither summits nor
  explores still sees one.
  Birds roost once the sun is a couple of degrees below the horizon.
  Darkness defers a pass the way an obscured sky does, so a single flock
  crosses after sunrise rather than a night's worth arriving together, and a
  flock overtaken by nightfall fades out with the light.

  Shooting stars. First due at 20 active seconds, then 20 seconds after each
  launch. A varied path is chosen in the current camera view, above the
  terrain. If the sky is obscured the launch waits without accumulating a
  backlog; once launched, the world-space path stays fixed for its
  1.15-second duration.

  Hidden tabs stop all of these clocks. Reduced motion hides birds and
  shooting stars.

Screen wake lock
  Automatic travel requests a screen wake lock on supporting secure browsers.
  Pause, manual navigation, hidden tabs, and summit arrival release it.
  Returning to the tab reacquires it only if automatic travel is still
  active. The summit panorama does not keep the display awake. Unsupported
  browsers, battery-saving denials, and OS revocation are handled quietly
  without a repeated retry loop. Unit tests mock the browser API; verify
  actual dimming behaviour on a phone.

Maintenance and SEO
  Content and metadata are delivered in index.html without JavaScript, and
  the default readable layout also handles unavailable JavaScript or WebGL.
  A canonical URL, description, Open Graph/Twitter metadata, and ProfilePage
  structured data identify the public site at https://prajwalit.com/.
  images/social-preview.jpg supplies the landscape preview for shared links.
  images/profile-pic.png introduces the about dialog; it is lazy, so visitors
  who never open the dialog never download it. The legacy social icons and
  the retained Trolltunga photo have been removed; both remain in git history.
  robots.txt advertises sitemap.xml; update both if the production domain
  changes. Keep the local Three.js license and Water customizations when
  updating the vendor.

Validation
  Requires Node.js 20 or newer; no npm install or build step is needed.
  npm test
  npm run check
  git diff --check
  The dependency-free Node tests cover panorama continuity, journey controls,
  reduced motion, panning, audio and storage behaviour, the aurora window,
  bird and shooting-star scheduling, vegetation placement, wallpaper export,
  WebGL fallback, refresh reset, metadata, sitemap consistency, and local
  asset and anchor references.
  Browser boundaries are mocked; these tests do not validate GPU rendering,
  browser autoplay policy, CSS layout, or actual search-engine indexing.
  Preview the five chapters, sound controls, dialogs, and the summit pan.
  Also check with JavaScript disabled and at a narrow viewport.
